# MCP Safety

Treat MCP servers as trusted integrations with real access to tools and data.

- Prefer example configuration files over auto-writing live `mcp.json`.
- Use `${env:NAME}` placeholders for secrets and credentials.
- Avoid hardcoded absolute paths when `${workspaceFolder}` or `${userHome}` can
  express the same intent.
- Keep server scope narrow and explain why each server is installed.
- Review whether a project needs project-local MCP or user-global MCP.

## Approval policy

If an MCP tool can mutate external systems, require clear intent and review the
server's permissions before enabling auto-run behavior.
